    "step5_reality_check": {
      "0": "Slack’s global search UI is visible as a modal dialog with the scope text “Search across people, channels, files, workflows, and more,” indicating workspace-wide search capability. Screenshot evidence explicitly shows a executed search for “errorissue” (results page says “Results for: errorissue” with “Nothing turned up”) and shows “error” and “errorissue” in Recent searches. Recent searches also include entries like “issueDaytona,” but the screenshots do not explicitly show separate completed searches for the exact standalone terms “issue,” “failed,” or “help.”",
      "1": "Search results lists are shown for at least the query “error,” including many substantive, timestamped results and pagination (“Message results, page 1 of 2” and “page 2 of 2”). The UI also shows “Sort: Most relevant (default),” so the on-screen ordering is not explicitly “most recent” unless changed (no screenshot shows a switch to a time-based sort). Evidence shows multiple recent-looking items (e.g., “Yesterday at 2:29 AM,” “Aug 27th”), but screenshots alone don’t prove the agent opened/inspected exactly the 5 most recent items, nor that similar inspection occurred for the terms “issue,” “failed,” and “help.”",
      "2": "No screenshot evidence shows any combined cross-term output or side-by-side comparison of results across multiple queries. The Slack UI shown includes separate query contexts (e.g., “Results for: error” vs “Results for: issue”), but there is no visible mechanism in the screenshots indicating deduplication handling (e.g., a merged list, message IDs, or an agent-produced consolidated table), so deduplication cannot be directly grounded in the provided visuals.",
      "3": "Thread panels are shown as open and readable for at least some search hits. One screenshot shows a thread for “My sandbox encountered an unrecoverable error” with a visible staff reply requesting details via support chat. Another screenshot clearly shows an “issue” thread about files appearing as nobody:nogroup with multiple replies, including investigation, a ‘good to go’ message, reporter confirmation, and a closing note. Other screenshots show many results with reply counts/buttons (e.g., “4 replies,” “5 replies,” “6 replies”), but the screenshots only directly evidence opening/reading some specific threads, not all threaded results.",
      "4": "The screenshots only show Slack’s search results pages and thread panes (where author, channel, timestamp, snippets, and sometimes full thread replies are visible). There is no screenshot of any agent-produced final output table. Thus, while the UI provides the raw fields needed for table columns (term context, channel, author, timestamp, message snippet, and thread replies), the actual presence/structure/completeness of the required final table output is not visible in the provided evidence.",
      "5": "At least one thread contains explicit resolution language visible in screenshots: the nobody:nogroup thread includes the reporter saying “Thanks, it’s working now!” and support stating they will close the thread, which is explicit evidence supporting a ‘resolved’ label for that item. Other shown threads (e.g., unrecoverable error reply asking to contact support chat; another thread mentioning a fix merged/deploying and asking to check) do not, in the visible excerpts, contain explicit ‘resolved’ confirmation. Screenshots do not show any agent-created status labels across all rows, so only the availability (or absence) of explicit resolution phrases in specific visible threads can be grounded.",
      "6": "Multiple screenshots show message composition/reply inputs and reaction affordances (e.g., a thread reply box with an “Also send to …” option; a channel message composer with “Send now”; ‘react with … emoji’ controls). However, no screenshot shows a sent message, an edit/delete indicator, or a reaction being applied as a result of the agent’s action—only the presence of UI controls that could modify content. Therefore, the evidence supports that modification was possible in the UI, but does not show any explicit modification occurring."
    },
